/**
\fn uint32 pdg::Sprite::getSerializedSize(ISerializer* serializer) const
\brief Measure selected Sprite state and its resource payloads.
\param serializer Writer used to size shared resources and select their save policy; not null.
\return Record size in bytes, excluding the stream header and enclosing object tag/length.
Uses the owning layer's serialization flags, or `ser_Full` off-layer. Initial
records validate supported state and image availability during sizing.
\sa Sprite::serialize(), SpriteLayer::setSerializationFlags()
*/

/**
\fn void pdg::Sprite::serialize(ISerializer* serializer) const
\brief Write selected Sprite state to a data stream.
\param serializer Destination writer; not null.
The owning layer selects state flags; an off-layer Sprite uses `ser_Full`.
Initial animation/image records include frame artwork, collision masks, frame
layout and fractional playback position. Shared images remain shared. The writer's
ISerializer::setResourceMode() selects embedded pixels or external image references.
Corresponding state flags include programmed transforms/tweens and PhysicsBody
state, including active drives. Timing uses floating-point seconds.

Updates with `ser_Animations` (including `ser_Update`) transfer sampled local Part
transforms, reflection, constant movement and scheduled animations by Part ID.
They preserve existing Part identities, parent links and artwork. This includes
poses already solved by Part IK. The receiver can stop Layer animations and
disable collisions to display only the received state. Part creation/removal,
reparenting, artwork changes, IK controller configuration and Part body/constraint
state require another initial snapshot; incremental Part records are not full
physics checkpoints. A changed Part ID set is rejected.


Initial records also preserve independent Parts: IDs/names, parent links, local
transforms, paused/active tweens, bodies and active drives. Part Image/Drawing
content retains geometry, attributes, nested drawings and shared images. Drawing
content is embedded in both modes; its images follow the selected resource policy.

Drawing colors and image transparency colors use eight-bit channels, with opaque
alpha omitted. Floating-point colors can change by less than one channel step
(1/255) when restored. Image pixel data is preserved exactly.
Drawing geometry, pixel-space attributes (including thickness, text size, offsets
and transform translation), and image subsections use the compact coordinate
serializers. These can discard a fractional remainder smaller than 0.001 per
encoded coordinate component. Rectangles encode their origin and size, so those
small errors can accumulate at the opposite edge. Physics state, collision
geometry, constraint anchors, IK inputs and Sprite/Part transforms retain their
existing floating-point precision.

Packed boolean flags omit default values and absent payloads. Repeated images,
Drawing storage and imported animation assets use shared object references within
the same serializer stream. This preserves shared identity and avoids writing
the same resource again; independently created objects remain distinct.


Imported SCML animations retain their shared model, selected entity/clip,
floating-point playback time, pause/events, character maps, reference pose, bone
overrides, blends and interrupted transitions. Bone-, artwork- and socket-bound
Parts resolve against the restored rig. Built-in rig IK modifiers and retained
Drawing registrations preserve their IDs, options and enabled state. Animation
colliders continue following the restored pose.

Complete saves embed the imported document and its images and can load without
the source files. Embedded XML is compressed losslessly with zlib when that makes
it smaller; decompression is automatic. Documents are limited to 64 MiB before
compression, and corrupt payloads or mismatched lengths cause a load error.
External-reference saves omit the XML, resolve the original document path and
verify that its contents have not changed; images use the selected resource policy.
Missing or changed dependencies cause a load error.

Initial records preserve Collider shapes and shape IDs, frame/animation-source options,
material overrides, collision filters, sensor/enabled state and contact-event
opt-in. Compound-body associations, all PhysicsConstraint types, linked IK limits
and active physical Part IK controllers resolve after all owners exist. Captured
IK offsets and sampled drives survive; offsets are not recaptured from a deformed
pose. Geometry is stored in both resource modes; frame images follow the resource
policy. Restored colliders receive new process-wide IDs. Body angular-speed break
thresholds, reference identities and notification latches also survive. Relative
references must belong to the saved graph; a reported excursion does not repeat
on restore. Native PhysicsBody break callbacks must be cleared before saving.

Initial records also preserve active physical rigs created from explicit
configurations or generated skeletons. They save edited body/collider/constraint
components, bone mappings, root selection, attached/detached membership, aggregate
mass, desired poses, Kinematic/Dynamic/Driven modes, manual drive targets, rig
drive settings and recovery progress. A pending recovery or disable resumes with
its remaining duration and completion notification; a completed recovery is not
reported again. Load into a Chipmunk-capable build and use a physics-enabled
layer to continue simulation. A standalone restored Sprite can be inspected
before adding it to a layer. These records preserve engine state, not application
callbacks; re-register application logic after loading. Save at an unlocked
physics boundary. Layer initial restores validate the staged graph before
replacing the live scene. Incremental motion updates are not rig checkpoints.

Callback collision filters/handlers are rejected; reinstall application handlers
after loading. Solver contact caches and pending contact events are not saved,
so loading rebuilds contacts and can produce new Begin events. A resumed simulation
is not guaranteed to be bit-for-bit identical to an uninterrupted one.

\note Initial snapshots support frame-based and imported Sprites with independent
or authored Parts.
Standalone Sprite saves reject attachments; save their owning SpriteLayer to retain
mount relationships and cross-Sprite physical links. Older attachSprite() associations, modifier/drawing callbacks, callback helpers,
custom easing, Font attributes and custom Drawing implementations are rejected. Drawing content requires a GUI-capable build. Layer collections
can be restored with SpriteLayer::deserialize(). Discard the writer after a save error.

Use ISerializer::serialize_obj() and IDeserializer::deserialize_obj() to construct
a new Sprite automatically; the returned native object owns a reference that the
caller must release. Raw serialize()/deserialize() calls replace existing state.
\sa Sprite::deserialize(), Sprite::getSerializedSize()
*/

/**
\fn void pdg::Sprite::deserialize(IDeserializer* deserializer)
\brief Restore selected state from a Sprite record.
\param deserializer Reader positioned at a raw Sprite record; not null.
An initial record supplies its state flags and replaces frame images, masks and
playback position, retaining shared image resources. It replaces independent and authored Parts
after validating their identities, parent links, bindings, artwork and body state. Previously
retained Parts are detached; IDs removed before saving are not reused. Incremental updates use the
destination layer's serialization flags. Missing resources, malformed data and
unsupported formats throw. This is not an atomic scene load; discard the reader
and destination after a failed load.
\sa Sprite::serialize(), ISerializer::setResourceMode()
*/
